    {
     "text": ". Their 90th percentile is about $543K. That's only one company, but $552K is high by that measure. Two cautions:\n\n- **The base is ordinary.** The midpoint of the posted range is $227K, so $235K is only a little above it. You have $36K of room before the $271K cap.\n- **The $552K depends on the stock and on assumptions.** It assumes Halvern's share price stays flat and the bonus pays at target.\n\n## What you'd give up\n\nSince you're happy where you are, these things decide whether the move is worth it:\n\n1. **Unvested Varrowby equity.** If you've been there a few years, your refresh grants have stacked up. Your actual annual vesting may be well above your original grant, and you lose all of it when you leave.\n2. **Your 2026 bonus.** It's November. If Varrowby pays bonuses in Q1, leaving now probably means losing nearly a full year's bonus.\n3. **Refreshes start over.** At Halvern you'd have zero refreshes stacked in year 1, and they aren't guaranteed. Without them, your pay drops sharply in year 5, when the new-hire grant runs out.\n4. **The vesting schedule.** Ask whether there's a 1-year cliff, whether vesting is even or back-loaded, and how the grant is turned into shares (for example, a 30-day average price).\n\n**My rule of thumb:** Compare year by year, counting what Varrowby would realistically pay you in years 1\u20134, including grants you've already been given. Since you weren't looking, I'd want Halvern to come out at least 15\u201320% ahead to justify the risk of a new role and starting your tenure over. That threshold is my judgment, not a market standard. If Halvern can't get there, turning it down is a perfectly good outcome.\n\n## What to ask for\n\nYou have real leverage: they came to you, and you're comfortable staying. Ask for one package, all at once:\n\n1. **Sign-on bonus (the most likely win, since they offered none):** ask for your forfeited 2026 bonus plus the Varrowby equity that would have vested in the next 12 months. If that comes to $80K, ask for $80K. It can be split over two years. \"Make me whole\" is the easiest request for a company to approve.\n2. **Base:** ask for **$255K**. That's in the upper part of their posted range without being the cap. Expect to end up around $245\u2013250K.\n3. **RSUs:** ask for about **$1.25M**, roughly 15% more. Equity is often the most flexible part of the offer at public companies.\n\n**Script:** *\"I wasn't looking and I'm happy at Varrowby, but I'm excited about this role. To make the move, I'd need a $255K base, $1.25M in RSUs, and an $X sign-on to cover the bonus and equity I'd leave behind. If you can get there, I'm ready to sign.\"*\n\nDon't make up a competing offer. \"I'm happy staying\" is your leverage. Also ask the recruiter how big refreshes typically are for strong performers (as a percentage of the new-hire grant) and what Halvern's bonus payout has been historically.\n\n**To give you a firm accept or walk-away answer, send me:** your current base, target bonus, how much equity vests each year, your total unvested equity and its vesting schedule, and when Varrowby pays bonuses. With those, I can lay out a year-by-year comparison and size the sign-on ask exactly.",
